# Recent Progress in MOF-Derived, Heteroatom-Doped Porous Carbons as Highly Efficient Electrocatalysts for Oxygen Reduction Reaction in Fuel Cells

## abstract
Abstract: Currently, developing nonprecious‐metal catalysts to replace Pt‐based electrocatalysts in fuel cells has become a hot topic because the oxygen reduction reaction (ORR) in fuel cells often requires platinum, a precious metal, as a catalyst, which is one of the major hurdles for commercialization of the fuel cells. Recently, the newly emerging metal‐organic frameworks (MOFs) have been widely used as self‐sacrificed precursors/templates to fabricate heteroatom‐doped porous carbons. Here, the recent progress of MOF‐derived, heteroatom‐doped porous carbon catalysts for ORR in fuel cells is systematically reviewed, and the synthesis strategies for using different MOF precursors to prepare heteroatom‐doped porous carbon catalysts, including the direct carbonization of MOFs, MOF and heteroatom source mixture carbonization, and MOF‐based composite carbonization are summarized. The emphasis is placed on the precursor design of MOF‐derived metal‐free catalysts and transition‐metal‐doped carbon catalysts because the MOF precursors often determine the microstructures of the derived porous carbon catalysts. The discussion provides a useful strategy for in situ synthesis of heteroatom‐doped carbon ORR electrocatalysts by rationally designing MOF precursors. Due to the versatility of MOF structures, MOF‐derived porous carbons not only provide chances to develop highly efficient ORR electrocatalysts, but also broaden the family of nanoporous carbons for applications in supercapacitors and batteries.

Currently, developing nonprecious-metal catalysts to replace Pt-based electrocatalysts in fuel cells has become a hot topic because the oxygen reduction reaction (ORR) in fuel cells often requires platinum, a precious metal, as a catalyst, which is one of the major hurdles for commercialization of the fuel cells. Recently, the newly emerging metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) have been widely used as self-sacrificed precursors/templates to fabricate heteroatomdoped porous carbons. Here, the recent progress of MOF-derived, heteroatomdoped porous carbon catalysts for ORR in fuel cells is systematically reviewed, and the synthesis strategies for using different MOF precursors to prepare heteroatom-doped porous carbon catalysts, including the direct carbonization of MOFs, MOF and heteroatom source mixture carbonization, and MOF-based composite carbonization are summarized. The emphasis is placed on the precursor design of MOF-derived metal-free catalysts and transition-metal-doped carbon catalysts because the MOF precursors often determine the microstructures of the derived porous carbon catalysts. The discussion provides a useful strategy for in situ synthesis of heteroatom-doped carbon ORR electrocatalysts by rationally designing MOF precursors. Due to the versatility of MOF structures, MOF-derived porous carbons not only provide chances to develop highly efficient ORR electrocatalysts, but also broaden the family of nanoporous carbons for applications in supercapacitors and batteries.
## 1. Introduction
Currently, fast depletion of fossil fuels and deterioration of global warming caused by carbon dioxide emission largely stimulate the development of clean energy. As a new type of energy source, fuel cells have attracted wide attention owing to their high efficiency, environmental friendliness, and high reliability.[1] The performance of fuel cells is determined by oxygen reduction reaction (ORR) that occurs on the cathode, because the rate of ORR is several orders slower than hydrogen oxidation reaction in the anode, and it severely depends on the Pt-based materials as electrocatalysts for both reactions. The scarcity and high cost of Pt as well as declining issue of activity greatly hinder commercial applications of the fuel cells.[2] To break through these bottlenecks, recent researches were focused on rational design and synthesis of metal-free or transition metal-doped porous carbon electrocatalysts to replace commercial $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ .[3]
Since Jasinski found that cobalt phthalocyanine possesses the ORR activity in alkaline electrolytes in 1964,[4] the nonprecious metal ORR electrocatalysts have been widely reported by carbonizing transition metal macrocyclic compounds,[3b,5] conductive polymers,[6] the mixtures of transition metal salts, and $\mathrm{N}/\mathrm{C}$ sources.[7] A large number of experiments and simulations demonstrated that the $\scriptstyle\mathrm{M-N-C}$ (M is transition metal, say, Fe Co, and Cu) is the active site of transition metaldoped porous carbon catalysts.[8] As one of the most promising substitutes, the metal-doped porous carbon with Fe–N–C configuration exhibits excellent ORR performance both under alkaline and acidic conditions. Partial literature indicated that transition metals, e.g., Fe and Co, can act as a catalyst to form the ordered carbon structures, such as graphene and carbon nanotube (CNT).[9] However, the complicated structure caused by the high-temperature pyrolysis seriously hampers the exploration of the active sites for the ORR performance of the carbonized materials.
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), as a novel porous material, have received wide attention in recent years.[10] Compared to the traditional porous materials, MOFs show some specific advantages, such as the structural diversity, easily functionalized, high specific surface area, and good designability,[11] and, therefore, are extensively applied in gas adsorption and storage,[12] electrocatalysis,[13] sensors, etc.[14] Recently, MOFs have also been selected as self-sacrificed precursor/template for the preparation of nanoporous carbons because metal elements and organic compounds in MOFs can be in situ transformed into metal/metal oxide/metal carbide-loaded porous carbons and even metal-free nitrogen-doped porous carbons by pyrolysis. As a result, these as-synthesized samples can inherit the structure property of corresponding MOFs.[15] Compared to the traditional methods to prepare metal supported carbon catalysts, the MOF-derived porous carbon catalysts possess not only uniform active sites $(\mathrm{FeN}_{x},$ $\mathrm{CoN}_{x},$ and N–C), but also large specific surface area. Moreover, the resulting 3D ordered porous structure is favorable for the mass transfer and electron transport.
Since Liu and co-workers adopted MOF-derived porous carbon as electrocatalysts for ORR applications in 2011, different types of MOF-derived porous carbon catalysts have been widely reported. Several important events are shown in Figure 1. Liu and co-workers[16] selected the cobalt imidazolate skeleton structure as a precursor to produce cobaltdoped porous carbons by pyrolysis and found that the catalyst shows high ORR performance in acidic media. Then, Dodelet and co-workers synthesized the Fe, N-codoped porous carbon catalysts by carbonizing the mixture of iron salt, nitrogen source, and ZIF-8, in which the ZIF-8 served as a microporous host for storage of active sites. The catalyst-based polymer electrolyte membrane fuel cells produce a power density, comparable with that of the state-of-the-art Pt/C.[17] In addition to the MOF-derived metal-doped porous carbon catalysts mentioned above, our group first used ZIF-7/glucose as precursors to synthesize nitrogen-doped porous carbon catalyst, a kind of metal-free electrocatalyst with excellent ORR performance.[18] Then, to further improve the ORR activity of the catalysts, different structure MOF precursors (such as bimetallic MOFs, MOF/secondary heteroatom source mixtures and MOF composites, such as MOF/carbon nanotube, MOF/reduced graphene oxide (rGO), MOF/layered double hydroxide (LDH), MOF/ZnO, and MOF/Te nanowires) have been designed.[19] It is noticed that these strategies could efficiently synthesize high active electrocatalysts with more uniform active site (e.g., $\mathrm{FeN}_{x},$ $\mathrm{CoN}_{x},$ and N–C) and high conductivity.
In this review, we organize the contents as follows. First, we summarize the synthesis methods of MOF-derived porous carbon catalysts. Then, we systematically discuss the applications of MOF-derived metal-free porous carbon catalysts and nonprecious metal-doped porous carbon catalysts in ORR. Finally, some challenges and perspectives in the field are also addressed.
