## 2. Synthesis Methods of MOF-Derived Porous Carbon Catalysts
## 2.1. Carbonization Conditions
The synthesis of MOF-derived porous carbon catalysts generally consists of two main procedures: design of the precursors and control of the carbonization conditions. Porous carbon catalysts derived from MOFs must undergo a high-temperature pyrolysis. High temperature can improve the degree of graphitization and further increase the conductivity of catalysts to enhance ORR activity. However, the high temperature will reduce the amount of heteroatoms doping (e.g., nitrogen), which decreases the number of active sites. Therefore, the determination of a suitable temperature is a key factor to prepare the high efficient porous carbon catalysts derived from different MOF precursors.[20] Generally, to synthesize metalfree porous carbon catalysts, the $Z\mathrm{n}$ -containing MOFs are often adopted, and the pyrolysis temperature is set to higher than $907~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , which is beneficial for complete removal of $Z\mathrm{n}$ metal by its evaporation.[18] For the synthesis of transition metal-doped carbon catalysts, the carbonization temperature often covers a wide range of $700{-}1000\ {^{\circ}}\mathrm{C}$ , in which the metal doping not only provides a source of metal for the synthesis of highly active $(\mathrm{FeN}_{x}/\mathrm{CoN}_{x})$ catalysts, but also increases the degree of graphitization of the material, because transition metals are good graphitizing catalysts.[19a,21] To maintain the MOF structure in the process of carbonization in presence of the metals of Fe and Co, mesoporous-silica $\mathrm{(mSiO_{2})}$ -protected calcination strategy or MOF composite strategy is highly efficient.[22]
The carbonization atmosphere is another important factor to synthesize porous carbon catalysts derived from MOFs. Generally, it consists of inert gases $(\Nu_{2}$ , Ar), $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ , $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , etc.[23] The role of inert gas is to block off air to guarantee heteroatom doped into carbon matrix at high temperature, while ${\mathrm{NH}}_{3}$ atmosphere is beneficial for doping nitrogen into carbon matrix. $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ can catalyze metal reduction or oxide so that morphology and active sites of the catalysts can be controlled. For example, metallic Co can promote the formation of nanotube, while ${\mathrm{CoO}}_{x}/{\mathrm{FeO}}_{x},$ ${\mathrm{CoS}_{x}}/{\mathrm{FeS}_{x}}$ can provide active sites for bifunctional or multifunctional applications of catalysts.
## 2.2. Design of MOF Precursors
In addition to the carbonization condition, the design of the precursors is also a crucial step to prepare high efficient catalysts. The preparation of MOF generally undergoes the processes of coordination and self-assembly of the metal ions and organic ligands to form a cyclical skeleton porous structure. Synthetic methods of MOF include in situ hydrothermal synthesis, room temperature stirring, microwave synthesis, and seed crystal growth, which provide layouts to design and prepare different morphologies of MOFs (3D mesh, 2D film, etc.).[24] In order to achieve versatility of MOF precursors, MOF composite precursors were also designed.[25] The MOF composites were prepared by in situ growth on designed templates, such as CNT, rGO, LDH, cobalt carbonate hydroxide, and cobalt oxalate, through the introduction of some oxygen-containing functional groups or charged templates. In addition, the postsynthesis treatment was also used, i.e., adding secondary heteroatom sources to asprepared MOFs precursors through solid-phase or liquid-phase mixing.[26]
## 2.3. Synthesis Strategy of Porous Carbon Catalysts from Different MOF Precursors
From the above two procedures, we divided MOF-derived carbon catalysts into three categories according to the difference of MOFs precursors. The simplest one is to use MOFs as precursors to prepare porous carbon catalysts by a direct carbonization process (i.e., direct carbonization method). The second method is to use MOFs and secondary heteroatom sources as precursors to prepare porous carbon catalysts (i.e., MOF-heteroatom source mixture carbonization method). The third method is to use MOF composites (for example, MOF/ CNT, MOF/rGO, MOF/Te nanowire, etc.) as precursors to prepare porous carbon catalysts (i.e., MOF-based composite carbonization method). A detailed description is shown in Figure 2.
## 2.3.1. Direct Carbonization Method
The direct carbonization method has been widely applied in synthesis of MOF-derived porous carbon catalysts,[27] in which the involved MOFs include IR-MOF series,[28] MIL-101 (MIL-53) series,[29] PCN-222 series,[30] ZIF series,[31] HKUST-1 series,[32] etc. Using the method, investigators can conveniently control the size and morphology of the resulting samples, which is beneficial for analyzing the active sites of as-synthesized electrocatalysts. However, the electrocatalytic properties of the samples are often limited by the atom type and the structure of MOF templates. As a result, the ORR performance of the as-synthesized sample is generally weaker than commercial $20\%$ $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ . To extend the atom type limitation of an MOF precursor, bimetallic MOF materials, such as $Z{\mathrm{n}}/{\mathrm{Co}}$ , Zn/Fe, and Fe/Co bimetallic MOFs, were also designed as precursors to prepare the porous carbon catalysts via direct carbonization.[25b,33] Due to the synergetic effect of bimetal doping, the ORR activities of bimetallic MOF-derived porous carbon catalysts are often better than that of single Zn (or Co, Fe) containing MOF-derived ones. Previous investigations also indicated that $Z\mathrm{n}$ does not work for ORR,[18,34] but the evaporation of Zn at high temperature greatly increases the specific surface area of the catalysts, which is in favor of the electron and $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ transportation.
## 2.3.2. MOF-Heteroatom Source Mixture Carbonization Method
To improve the ORR activity of MOF-derived porous carbon catalysts, the MOF-heteroatom source carbonization method has been widely used. By adding secondary heteroatom sources to MOFs as precursors, the external heteroatomdoped porous carbon catalysts can be prepared rationally, which is not limited to the atom types in MOFs.[35] The externally introduced heteroatom sources include nonmetal sources (such as N,[36] P,[37] S,[36,38] and $\mathrm{C}^{[39]}$ ) and metal sources (Fe, Co, Cu, etc.). The major secondary nonmetal dopants contain furfural, chitosan, glucose, sucrose, glycerol, xylitol, carbon tetrachloride, glucose, ethylenediamine, urea, melamine, dicyandiamide, cyanamide, thiourea, acetonitrile, thioacetamide, triarylphosphine, dimethyl sulfoxide, and so on. Incorporating greatly different electronegativity nonmetal atoms (nitrogen: 3.04; phosphorus: 2.19; sulfur: 2.58; fluorine: 3.98; boron: 2.04) into carbon matrix can effectively tailor the distribution of the electronic and charges density of carbon materials, indicating that electronegativity of the nonmetal atoms induces the positive charge to the surrounding carbon atoms and further promotes the ORR performance.[40]
In addition, by introducing external metal sources (Fe, Co, etc.) to MOFs, Fe/Co–N–C porous carbon catalysts can be synthesized.[21c,f,41] A small amount of metal doping would make the Fe/Co–N–C active site exposed evenly without aggregation, which can efficiently boost the ORR performance. The strategy exhibits similar effect with bimetallic MOF templates. However, the external metal sources are not limited to the metal atoms in MOFs. Therefore, the introduction of the external metal source is relatively more versatile.
## 2.3.3. MOF-Based Composite Carbonization Method
As well known, the porous carbon catalysts derived from MOF precursors at high temperatures often suffer some disadvantages, such as the skeleton collapse of MOFs at the high temperatures $({>}700~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ and the pore shrinking.[15a,b,26,42] Interestingly, the MOF-based composite precursors, for example MOF/CNT, MOF/graphene, MOF/Te nanowires, MOF/cobalt carbonate hydroxide, MOF/cobalt oxalate, etc., are able to overcome the disadvantages to obtain the catalysts.[34,43] For example, coating a layer of carbon material on the surfaces of MOFs or combining the MOF with a suitable templating agent can efficiently prevent the skeleton of the MOF material from collapse, which means that the MOF-composite-derived porous carbon catalysts can maintain the high Brunauer-EmmettTeller (BET) specific surface area (SSA) and robust porosity, leading to a better conductivity and graphitization degree of the synthesized samples. As a result, it greatly improves the ORR activity of the samples.
Currently, the three methods described above are common strategies of the preparation of MOF-derived heteroatomdoped porous carbon catalysts. Next, we would summarize the recent advances of several kinds of MOF-derived electrocatalysts, i.e., metal-free carbon catalysts and Co, Fe-based porous carbon catalysts, obtained by the three methods mentioned above.
## 3. MOF-Derived Metal-free Heteroatom-Doped Porous Carbon Catalysts
Previous investigations have mentioned that metal-doped porous carbon materials have been proved to be highly active for ORR.[4] However, the metal-free porous carbons have been considered to be of inactivity or low activity for ORR until Dai and co-workers found that the N-doped vertical carbon nanotube arrays displayed excellent ORR catalytic activity in 2009,[44] which provided a solid basis for the development of the metal-free heteroatom-doped porous carbon catalysts. Compared to the metal-doped porous carbons, the metalfree heteroatom-doped porous carbons exhibit several merits. First, the synthesis method of the metal-free carbon catalysts is relatively simple and easy in operation. Second, the metalfree carbon catalysts deliver less environmental pollution for the absence of metals. Third, the metal-free carbon catalysts display better stability and antimethanol properties, and prevent the cross-effects against the metal-doped porous carbon catalysts.
## 3.1. Nitrogen-Doped Carbon Catalysts
Our group presented the first example of synthesis of the metal-free ORR electrocatalyst,[18] in which we used ZIF-7 and glucose mixture as a precursor to obtain the nitrogendoped carbon catalysts by carbonization at $950~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ The synthesis scheme is shown in Figure 3a. The synthesized nitrogen-doped porous carbon catalysts exhibited excellent ORR activities with the onset and half-wave potentials of 0.86 and $0.7\mathrm{V},$ and the limiting current of $4.6\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ (Figure 3b). After $50~000$ cycles of CV sweeping under oxygen conditions, its catalytic properties were almost the same as the original sample, indicating that the catalyst possessed good stability (Figure 3c). Importantly, we found when Zn-MOFs/Zn-ZIFs are used as precursors, evaporation etching of Zn reduced by carbon can act as an activation agent for the formation of micropores in the resulting porous carbons, because the carbonization temperature of $950~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in the synthesis process is higher than the boiling point $(908^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ of $Z\mathrm{n}$ , which removes Zn completely and the metal-free N-doped porous carbon can be thus obtained. Moreover, introduction of the extra carbon source glucose can further promote the ORR activity of the samples, because the premelting and polymerization of glucose on the surface of ZIF-7 in the range of $200{-}300~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ further helps zinc ion reduction at the high temperatures, producing the final sample of a metal-free catalyst. Further analysis also indicated that pyridine nitrogen is beneficial for the ORR activity.
Zhang et al. subsequently reported a nitrogen-doped porous carbon catalyst (NGPC-1000-10) with the high BET specific surface area by directly carbonizing ZIF-8 at 1000 $^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{10\mathrm{~h~}}$ . It is believed that, during the decomposition of MOFs, the structure collapse at elevated temperatures restricts its utilization as a true hard template for carbon formation. However, as evident from the transmission electron microscopy (TEM) images of NGPC-1000-10 catalyst (see Figure 4a–d), the carbonized samples partially maintained original ZIF-8 morphology, which confirms the role of ZIF-8 as an ideal template for carbonization. The onset and half-wave potential of the carbon catalyst are weaker (40 mV) than the commercial $20\%\ \mathrm{Pt/C}$ (see Figure 4e), but it shows excellent cycling stability and antimethanol crossover (see Figure 4f).[15b]
Pandiaraj et al. also adopted MOF-5 and melamine as a precursor to synthesize nitrogen-doped porous carbon catalysts via the two-step pyrolysis, in which the first-step carbonization of the MOF was performed at $1000~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and the second-step carbonization at $900~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ by adding melamine as extra nitrogen source.[26b] The electrocatalyst exhibits better ORR activity than the sample of directly carbonizing MOF-5, but they are still inferior to the commercial $20\%$ $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ . Xu and co-workers used N-rich ZIF-8 as the template, and furfuryl alcohol and $\mathrm{NH_{4}O H}$ as the secondary $\mathrm{N}/\mathrm{C}$ sources to prepare the nitrogen-decorated porous carbon catalysts. The ORR onset potential of as-synthesized sample NC-900 reaches $0.83\mathrm{~V~}$ in the alkaline medium.[45] Then, Zhang et al. synthesized nitrogen-doped porous carbon/carbon nanotube by the three-step carbonization of MOF-5.[46] These resulting hybrids display excellent ORR performance with slightly more positive activities on onset potential $(-51~\mathrm{mV}$ vs $\mathrm{\Ag/AgCl}_{\mathrm{\ell}};$ ) and half-wave potential $(-171~\mathrm{~mV})$ than those of $20\%$ $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ which are mainly attributed to the synergistic contributions of its carbon/carbon nanotube unique hybrid structure, rich nitrogen doping, high graphitic degree, and large surface area. Most recently, Li et al. also adopted a $Z\mathrm{n}$ -containing MOF as a precursor to synthesize the pyridinic-N-doped porous carbon catalyst and found that its high ORR activity is attributed to the high density pyridinic-N sites and the decreased electron impedance.[47]
Importantly, Kondo and co-workers designed and synthesized a layered model catalyst with uniform $\pi$ structure and precise control of doping nitrogen type,[48] and further revealed that the contribution of ORR activity is from the Lewis basic carbon atom neighboring the pyridine nitrogen, which provides a solid basis for the further development of highly active catalysts. All above investigations indicate that incorporating nitrogen atoms into porous carbons can markedly enhance the ORR performance of catalysts.
## 3.2. Multi-Nonmetallic Heteroatom-Doped Carbon Catalysts
In addition to nitrogen doping, other nonmetal elements, phosphorus, sulfur, boron, fluorine, and so on, can also act as a dopant to modify porous carbon electrocatalysts via diatom or multiatom codoping manners.[15a] Codoping of the atoms with different electronegativities in the carbon matrix would break the electroneutrality of the porous carbon surface and achieve the redistribution of surface charge of the porous carbons. Compared to the single-atom doping, the codoping of the different nonmetal atoms in porous carbons would lead to the synergistic effect induced by the surface charge redistribution of porous carbons. Recently, our research group[49] used the phosphorus-doped $\mathrm{UiO-}66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ as a precursor through postsynthetic modification to prepare a series of phosphorus–nitrogen-codoped porous carbon catalysts by one-step pyrolysis at different temperatures (see Figure 5a). As shown in Figure 5b, the onset and half-wave potentials of the N, P-codoped carbons are 0.92 and $0.8~\mathrm{V},$ respectively, which are close to $20\%\ \mathrm{Pt/C}$ . The results indicate that the N, P-codoped catalysts give better ORR performance than the nitrogendoped ones due to the charge redistribution induced by the difference of electronegativity between the doped atoms and the carbon atoms, which creates the charged sites (say, ${\mathrm{C}}^{+},{\mathrm{P}}^{+}$ ) that are favorable for $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ adsorption and further facilitate the ORR process.
Besides $\mathrm{\DeltaP}$ and N codoping, the S, N-codoped porous carbon was also synthesized by MOF-5 as template, urea as nitrogen source, and dimethyl sulfoxide as sulfur source.[26a] The resulting metal-free electrocatalyst shows the positive onset potential $(-0.005\mathrm{~V~}$ vs $\mathrm{\Ag/AgCl]}$ and is even comparable to the commercial $20\%\ \mathrm{Pt/C}$ for ORR. Li et al. used MOF-5 as a template to synthesize N, P, S ternary-doped metal-free porous carbon for ORR. Due to the synergistic effect of N, P, and S ternary doping, the catalyst shows a high onset potential as a metal-free electrocatalyst for ORR among the currently reported metal-free electrocatalysts, very close to the commercial $20\%$ Pt/C catalyst.[15a]
## 3.3. MOF-Based Composite Carbon Catalysts
By using MOF-based composites (MOF/CNT, MOF/graphene, MOF/Te nanowires, MOF/cobalt carbonate hydroxide, MOF/cobalt oxalate, etc.) as precursors, the synthesized metalfree heteroatom-doped carbons possess some specific advantages, as mentioned previously. Liu et al. coated ZIF-8 on the surface of graphene and used the ZIF-8/graphene composite as a precursor to prepare N-doped porous carbon/graphene catalysts via pyrolysis (Figure 6). The N-doped sample shows slightly better ORR activity (the onset potential is 1.01 V, and the half-wave potential is 0.8 V) than the commercial $20\%$ $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ (Figure 6d). In addition, the OER performance of the catalyst is also comparable to commercial $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ and significantly better than commercial $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ catalyst.[50] To achieve the synergistic effect, homogenously coating ZIF-8 on GO is of great importance for the formation of abundant nitrogen-doped carbon on graphene and their close contact produces a consecutive conductive network. Owing to good solubility, wideopen nature, and surface-rich O-containing functional groups (e.g., $-\mathrm{OH}$ and COOH), graphene oxide (GO) sheets can coordinate with ${\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ in reaction precursors to provide a reaction platform for the nucleation and growth of ZIF-8 nanocrystals on GO. The TEM analysis demonstrated that ZIF-8 $@$ GO sheet-like structure is uniformly dispersed, and ZIF-8 nanocrystals almost fully cover on both surfaces of GO sheets to form sandwich-like structured composite (see Figure 6c). Such a sandwich-like structure favorably prevents graphene sheet aggregation during high-temperature pyrolysis. As expected, the ZIF-8/GO composite-derived catalyst exhibits better ORR performance than the ZIF-8-derived one. Similarly, Zhong et al. also deposited the ZIF-8 on the graphene layer to prepare nitrogen-doped porous carbon by carbonization at $800~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.^{[51]}$ The catalyst produces a power density of $33.8~\mathrm{mV~cm^{-2}}$ in a fuel cell, which is even more effective than the platinum cell $(22.5\mathrm{mV}\mathrm{cm}^{-2})$ ).
To design a pronounced structure of the precursor to enhance the ORR performance, Zhang et al. used 1D Te nanowires $@$ ZIF-8 as a precursor to synthesize nitrogendoped porous carbon (Figure 7).[25a] The BET SSA of ZIF-Te1000 catalyst reaches $2270~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , and its half-wave potential of $-0.161$ V $\left(\mathrm{Ag\/AgCl}\right)$ ) is superior to $31~\mathrm{mV}$ than the commercial $20\%\:\:\mathrm{Pt/C}$ . It is well recognized that the 1D Te $@$ ZIF-8 composite precursor takes advantage of generating nanofibrous morphology for improving electron conduction and mass transport, because the resulting nitrogen-doped carbon catalyst maintains well the hollow hierarchical pore structure and nanofibrous morphology (see Figure 7c), due to vaporization of Te NWs at high temperature. Cao and coworkers prepared effective nitrogen-doped (N-doped) metalfree carbon nanotube electrocatalysts by using the core–shell $Z_{ Ḋ }\mathrm{nO@\bigcirc\mathrm{ZIF}}{\cdot}8$ nanorods as a precursor, and the catalyst exhibited excellent ORR performances with high stability due to the synergistic effects of N doping and unique porous nanotube morphology.[52]
It is realized that how to design MOF precursors to prepare the metal free heteroatom-doped porous carbons is still a great challenge. Currently, synthesis of metal-free carbon electrocatalysts is still limited to Zn-containing MOF or ZIF precursors. Moreover, the carbonization temperature must be higher than the boiling point of Zn. Otherwise, it is rather difficult to completely remove the metals of the resulting samples. Interestingly, as an analog family of MOFs, covalent organic materials, which are composed of light elements (H, B, O, C, Si, etc.) via strong covalent bonds and widely applied in gas storage,[53] catalysis,[54] and other fields, would be a better candidate precursor for the synthesis of metal-free carbon catalysts. As a result, a template-designable strategy for synthesis of nitrogen-doped porous carbon electrocatalysts for ORR was proposed.[40a] As an example, 2D covalent-organic polymers (COPs) were adopted with well-controlled locations of nitrogen atoms as a precursor to prepare the ORR electrocatalysts by pyrolysis at $950~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , where the nitrogen location and holey structure of COPs were in situ translated into the resultant porous carbon catalysts. The COP-derived nitrogen-doped porous carbon electrocatalysts exhibit similar ORR activity with commercial $20\%\ \mathrm{Pt/C}$ . Alternatively, using COFs or COPs as precursors to synthesize metal-free electrocatalysts would be an excellent strategy for in situ synthesis of metal-free electrocatalysts. Moreover, the carbonization temperature of COFs and COPs precursors can cover a wide range from 600 to 1000 $^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . This is beneficial not only for the structure preservation of designed precursors, but also for the control of size and morphology of the resulting samples. Moreover, the addition of nonmetal heteroatom sources into carbon nanotubes, carbon black, graphene, nanofibers, and carbon aerogels is another convenient way to synthesize nonmetal atom-doped porous carbon catalysts. For example, Dai and co-workers synthesized N, B-codoped graphene foams and further found that they show better electrocatalytic activity for ORR than their undoped counterparts.[55] Liao and co-workers prepared S- and N-codoped carbon catalysts by using polyacrylonitrile nanospheres and sulfur as precursor via pyrolysis, and the synthesized S, N-codoped catalysts presented good ORR performance in alkaline medium.[56]
## 4. MOF-Derived Metal-Doped Porous Carbon ORR Catalysts
In fact, MOFs as templates/precursors are favorable for the synthesis of metal-doped porous carbons, because the metal distributions in MOFs can be well maintained to achieve a good dispersion in the resulting porous carbons, and therefore improve the density of active sites.[57] It is the reason that MOF-derived metal-doped porous carbon electrocatalysts have received wide applications in ORR. Actually, a part of MOF-derived metal-doped porous carbon catalysts exhibit superior performances than commercial $20\%\ \mathrm{Pt/C}$ for ORR under alkaline conditions and also show excellent electrochemical properties under acidic conditions, which provides a very promising strategy for synthesis of the nonprecious metal-doped porous carbon ORR catalysts. It is recognized that the metal-doped porous carbon catalysts generally possess $\mathrm{M-N_{4}/M-N_{\it x}}$ (metal–nitrogen) structure, known as the catalytic active sites, in which cobalt and iron are often the centers of the macrocyclic compounds with excellent catalytic activity. Commonly, MOF materials are composed of metals and organic ligands. After carbonization, the metal distributions and locations in MOFs can be in situ maintained in the resultant samples.[58] Therefore, the MOF-derived metal/ metal oxide-doped porous carbon electrocatalysts have been extensively investigated, and the MOF-derived Co, Fe, Cubased doped porous carbon catalysts were summarized as below.
## 4.1. Direct Carbonization of MOFs
Wang et al. used Co-containing ZIF-67 as a template to prepare a Co–N–C porous carbon catalyst, and the resultant $\scriptstyle\mathrm{Co-N-C}$ catalyst exhibits excellent activity for ORR in both alkaline and acidic media.[59] Compared with the ZIF-8-derived porous carbon catalyst under the same condition, the superior performance of the ZIF-67-derived $\scriptstyle\mathrm{Co-N-C}$ catalyst may be attributed to the existence of active sites of ${\mathrm{Co-N-C}}$ bonds. In particular, the onset and half-wave potential of the $\scriptstyle\mathrm{Co-N-C}$ catalyst are 0.96 and $0.84\mathrm{~V~}$ under alkaline conditions, being slightly better than the commercial $20\%\ \mathrm{Pt/C}$ . As well known, the power density of the alkaline fuel cells is lower by several times than that of the proton exchange membrane fuel cells (PEMFCs). Recent commercialization of PEMFCs has received extensive attention. Meanwhile, the ORR performance of nonprecious metal electrocatalysts under acidic conditions is still a great challenge. As a result, the onset and half-wave potential of the Co–N–C catalyst are 0.85 and $0.71\mathrm{~V~}$ under acidic condition, respectively, which are still lower than the commercial $20\%$ $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ . Xia et al. explored the effects of different sizes $(1.7\upmu\mathrm{m}$ , ${800}\mathrm{nm}$ , and $300\mathrm{nm}$ ) of ZIF-67 precursor on the ORR activity of the resulting samples,[42d] and found that the small size $\cdot300\ \mathrm{nm})$ ZIF-67 precursor is in favor of obtaining high activity catalysts, because the shape of small size precursors can easily be maintained and avoid structural damage in the heat treatment, which thus leads to the exposure of more active sites and the rapid transport of oxygen and electrons. Zhang et al. used the Co–I–MOF precursor formed by cobalt salt and hypoxanthine to synthesize the $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{N-C}$ hybrid carbon catalyst. The catalyst exhibits excellent ORR electrocatalytic activities with a four-electron path and long-term stability in alkaline media.[60]
Besides cobalt mentioned above, iron is another metal dopant helpful for ORR.[61] A series of experiments show that Fe–N–C catalysts exhibit better electrochemical performance for ORR under acidic conditions than $\scriptstyle\mathrm{Co-N-C}$ catalysts.[62] Zhao et al. used MIL- ${\cdot88\mathrm{B-NH}_{3}}$ as a precursor to prepare an Fe-doped porous carbon catalyst by direct carbonization at $900^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The onset and half-wave potentials of the Fe-doped catalyst are 1.03 and $0.92~\mathrm{V},$ respectively, which are slightly superior to the commercial $20\%\ \mathrm{Pt/C}$ . Moreover, the power density of the Fe-doped catalyst-based fuel cell reaches $22.7~\mathrm{mW~cm}^{-2}$ which is 1.7 times higher than the commercial $20\%$ Pt/C-doped catalyst-based fuel cells.[63] Recently, Li et al. adopted a lamellar MOF formed by dicyanoimidazole and iron acetate as a precursor to prepare porous carbon catalysts by carbonization. The catalyst provides most active sites accessible to electrolyte and thus shows a superior ORR electrocatalytic activity and stability than commercial $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ , which is attributed to the mesoporous structure derived from the special lamellar MOF.[21e]
## 4.1.1. Effects of Carbonization Atmosphere
The electrocatalysts with different morphologies and ORR performances can be obtained from ZIF-67 by changing the annealing gas atmosphere. Xia et al. reported the direct synthesis of N-doped carbon nanotube frameworks (NCNTFs) derived from ZIF-67 under an $\mathrm{Ar/H}_{2}$ atmosphere.[21b] The asprepared hollow structure retains the well-defined morphology of the initial ZIF-67 particles and has hierarchical shells of interconnected crystalline NCNTs (Figure 8a,b). ZIF-67 particles not only serve as the C and N source for growth of NCNTs with the assistance of metallic Co, but also act as the self-sacrificial template for the framework morphology. The synthesized NCNTF material exhibits attractive electrocatalytic activity in terms of the onset and half-wave potentials for the ORR under alkaline condition, and the electrocatalyst also shows superior electrochemical OER performance compared to that of the $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ catalyst (Figure 8c,d). Similarly, Aijaz et al. constructed the $\mathrm{Co}/\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\mathrm{-PC}/\mathrm{CNT}$ s catalyst by pyrolysis of ZIF-67 precursor under hydrogen and oxygen atmospheres.[23b] The catalyst presents excellent catalytic performance both in OER and ORR with the half-wave potential of $0.8\mathrm{~V~}$ in ORR and the onset potential of $1.65\mathrm{~V~}$ in OER. In short, the MOF self-sacrificial precursor is advantageous for obtaining such nanostructured polyhedra with large surface area and high dispersion of metal/metal oxide nanoparticles. Recently, Sikdar et al. used the dicyanamide-containing $\mathrm{Co^{II}}$ -based twofold interpenetrated 3D MOF (Co–MOF-1) as a precursor to prepare the bifunctional oxygen catalysts. The pyrolysis of the Co–MOF-1 under Ar atmosphere yields a Co nanoparticle embedded N-doped carbon nanotube catalyst, while the pyrolysis of the Co–MOF-1 under a reductive $\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{Ar}$ atmosphere produces the ${\mathrm{Co}}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}@{\mathrm{Co}}$ core–shell nanoparticle encapsulated N-doped carbon nanotube $(\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}@C o/N C N T})$ catalyst. The $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}@C o/N C N T}$ exhibits superior electrocatalytic activities for both the ORR with a potential of $0.88{\mathrm{V}}$ at $-1\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ and the OER with a potential of $1.61\mathrm{V}$ at $10\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ .[64]
## 4.1.2. Design of Bimetallic MOF Precursors
To improve the ORR properties of the obtained catalysts, bimetallic MOF precursors have also been used because the bimetallic components in the resulting sample often exhibit the synergic effects for boosting ORR performance. You et al. adopted ZIF-67/ZIF-8 bimetallic MOFs as precursors to synthesize porous carbon catalysts (Figure 9a).[65] By tailoring composition and architecture (the ratio of Co and $Z\mathrm{n}$ ) of bimetallic MOF precursors, the Co–N–C can be partially in situ transferred into the carbonized samples (see Figure 9b–e). They found that the presence of initial $Z\mathrm{n}$ produces a spatial isolation of Co that suppresses its aggregation during pyrolysis. Furthermore, Zn evaporation at high temperature is helpful for the formation of micropore and mesopore, and therefore increases the BET SSA, which can also improve the ORR activity. It is well recognized that the small Co nanoparticle size, intense interaction between Co and N, high content of pyridinic and pyrrolic N, and large specific surface area synergistically produce excellent ORR activity of $\scriptstyle\mathrm{Co-N-C}$ catalyst. The optimal $\scriptstyle\mathrm{Co-N-C}$ catalyst exhibits remarkable ORR activity in $0.1\mathrm{~\textmu~}$ KOH with a half-wave potential of $0.871\mathrm{~V},$ which is $30~\mathrm{mV}$ more positive than that of the commercial $20\%\ \mathrm{Pt/C}$ (Figure 9f). Moreover, it also shows a comparable ORR activity with $20\%\ \mathrm{Pt/C}$ in acidic and neutral electrolytes (Figure $^{\mathfrak{g}_{\mathrm{g},\mathrm{h}}}$ ). Recently, Hu et al. employed the core–shell ZIF ${\cdot}8@\mathrm{ZIF-}67$ crystals as a precursor to synthesize a Co, N-codoped carbon nanopolyhedra catalyst. The catalyst exhibits remarkable ORR activity with a half-wave potential of $0.855\mathrm{V},$ which is attributed to the uniform distribution of ${\mathrm{Co-N}}_{x}$ active sites and hierarchically micro/mesoporous structure.[66]
Zhao et al. used Zn, Fe imidazolate (FeIM) framework bimetallic MOFs as a precursor to prepare electrocatalysts and the corresponding PEMFC.[42e] The catalyst-based PEMFC shows an onset potential of 0.977 V and its volumetric current density reaches $12\ensuremath{\mathrm{~A~}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{cm}}^{-3}$ at $0.8\mathrm{~V~}$ in a single cell test. Unlike porous ZIFs, FeIM precursor is a nonporous material. To overcome the shortcoming, combining FeIM with porous ZIF-8 as the precursor, where ZIF-8 provides both high N content and high surface area, is a good method for the synthesis of high efficient electrocatalysts. Liu et al. used $\mathrm{Zn/Fe}$ bimetallic ZIFs as precursors to synthesize an Fe–N–C hybrid with nitrogen-doped hierarchical porous carbons crossed by carbon nanotubes.[67] The resulting Fe–N–C hybrid electrocatalyst exhibits excellent ORR activity, with a half-wave potential of 0.77 V, which is comparable to that of the commercial $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ catalysts in acidic media. It also gives a half-wave potential of $0.88\mathrm{~V},\approx50~\mathrm{mV}$ more positive than that of $20\%$ $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ in alkaline solution. Another approach is to use dual-MOF pyrolysis to prepare iron carbide nanoparticle-embedded carbon nanotube assemblies. Starting from a novel MOF-in-MOF precursor consisting of a $Z\mathrm{n}$ -based MOF polyhedron host and many engulfed Fe-based MOF nanorods, a complex structured carbon catalyst with remarkable ORR activity can be constructed.[68] However, the Co, Fe-containing MOF is limited by the type of MOF materials, and the corresponding reports are thus relatively few.
## 4.2. Carbonization of MOFs and Heteroatoms Mixture
## 4.2.1. Addition of Nonmetal Atom Source
It is commonly believed that the formation of $_{\mathrm{M-N-C}}$ bond provides the active sites and improves ORR activity, compared to the N-doped porous carbon catalyst. It means that the addition of the secondary nonmetal sources to Fe/Co-containing MOFs does not obviously promote the ORR activity of the metal-doped porous carbon catalysts. Therefore, there are only several reports about the addition of nonmetallic source in $_{\mathrm{Fe/Co-MOFs}}$ for the preparation of metal-doped carbon catalysts. For example, Hu et al. adopted ZIF-67 as a template and thioacetamide as a sulfur source to synthesize a $\scriptstyle\mathbf{Co-C}\ @$ $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}$ porous carbon catalyst (see Figure 10a).[38a] These double-shelled $\mathrm{Co-C}@\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}$ nanocages can be obtained by strictly controlling reaction with thioacetamide, whose fine structure (Figure 10d,e) guarantees the confinement of reactants with a high instantaneous concentration in the complex interior, leading to a strong driving force for the ORR. Furthermore, the Co–C nanocages inside $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}$ shells act as the active centers for the ORR. The $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}$ shells as nanoreactors prevent the Co–C active centers from aggregation. The structural features allow the $\mathrm{Co-C}@\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}$ porous carbon catalyst to exhibit remarkable electrocatalytic activity, durability, and methanol tolerance capability toward the ORR (Figure 10g). Noticeably, its onset potential reaches $0.96\mathrm{~V~}$ and the limiting current is $4.5\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ (Figure 10f). In addition, the addition of sulfur source in Co-containing MOF can form cobalt sulfide bonds via annealing treatment of the mixture of ZIF-9 and sulfur powder to construct $\mathrm{CoS}_{1.097}$ -doped porous carbon.[69] The onset potential of the catalyst is $0.8~\mathrm{V},$ and the number of transfer electron is about 4.0. Recently, $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{Gu}}$ et al. synthesized an Fe, N-codoped catalyst by using the $\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}@\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{-MIL}\mathrm{-}101$ as the precursor and found that the $\mathrm{g}{\cdot}\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ as a support template plays a critical role in the formation of the special architecture with high surface area and rich $\mathrm{~N~}$ content. Moreover, the as-synthesized catalyst shows excellent ORR activity in the range of pH 0–14.[41c]
The introduction of phosphorus sources in MOF precursor is another strategy for boosting ORR activity of the obtained samples. The ZIF-67/ZIF-8 composites with different compositions were used as precursors to prepare the Co, N-codoped porous carbons (CNCo-20) via pyrolysis at $900^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .[33] To further improve the performance of the samples, phosphorus source was introduced into CNCo-20 by annealing triphenylphosphine. The P-CNCo-20 catalyst exhibits more positive onset potential $(-0.04\mathrm{~V~}$ vs $\sf{A g}/$ AgCl) and half-wave potential $(-0.12\mathrm{~V~}$ vs $\mathrm{\sfAg/AgCl)}$ as well as higher diffusion-limited current density of $6.0\ \mathrm{mA\cm^{-2}}$ against its counterparts (CNCo-20) and $20\%\ \mathrm{Pt/C}$ . The superb catalytic activity of P-CNCo-20 is attributed to the synergistic effects of high surface area, ${\mathrm{Co-N}}_{x}$ species, uniform dispersion of N, Co, and P dopants as well as hierarchical pore structure. Experiments show that the addition of phosphorus indeed improves the ORR performance, but the enhanced mechanism of the ORR activity through the introduction of phosphorus is still unclear.
## 4.2.2. Addition of Metal Atom Sources
Actually, investigators can use the direct carbonization method to obtain the relatively good ORR electrocatalysts by designing Fe-containing MOF precursors, because the active sites of $\mathrm{Fe/FeC}_{x},$ Fe–N–C can be in situ formed easily by the pyrolysis of Fe–MOF. Therefore, the addition of nonmetal atom sources in MOF precursors becomes insignificant, as discussed above. However, adding metal source into MOF precursors provides another important strategy. Generally, a small amount of metal (Fe, Co) as dopants added to $Z\mathrm{n}$ -containing MOFs can significantly enhance ORR activity of the resulting samples,[21a,d] because it would greatly increase the activity sites. For example, Li et al. synthesized atomically dispersed cobalt/iron–nitrogen species as ORR electrocatalysts by using the mixture of ZIF-8 and cobalt/iron salt as a precursor (Figure 11). High-angle annular dark field scanning transmission electron microscopy (HAADFSTEM) and XANES confirm the well dispersion of isolated iron atoms on N-porous carbon catalyst, which exhibits the higher ORR activity, compared to commercial $20\%\:\mathrm{Pt/C}$ under alkaline conditions (Figure 11g). This is the first example of MOF-derived single-atom dispersed iron catalyst, which is an important advance of MOF-derived porous carbon catalysts.[21f,41a]
Recently, Xiang and co-workers prepared a highly efficient iron and nitrogen-doped carbon ORR catalyst by adding the Fecontaining COPs to MOFs as a precursor. The special MOF template guaranteed the catalysts to have a homogeneously distributed metal/nitrogen active sites. Therefore, the as-synthesized sample shows a $16~\mathrm{mV}$ positive half-wave potential compared with the benchmarked $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ .[41b] By adding different amounts of $\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{3}{\cdot}9\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (1, 5, and $10\mathrm{wt\%}$ ) into IRMOF-3 as a precursor, Sun et al. synthesized a series of Fe-doped catalysts via carbonization at $900~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under inert gas atmosphere.[70] The addition of a small amount of Fe definitely improves the electrocatalytic performance of the resulting sample. Results indicate that addition of $1\%$ Fe is more efficient because a small amount $(1\%)$ of Fe could enhance the active sites without destructing the microstructure of original precursors significantly. Moreover, the X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy results indicated that the catalyst with the addition of small amount of Fe possesses much higher nitrogen content compared to the samples without the addition of iron, which means that the Fe doping amounts significantly affect the N contents in the resulting carbon materials and therefore affect its ORR activity. Wu et al. also fabricated Fe, N-codoped porous carbon catalysts by pyrolyzing the bimetallic $\mathrm{Fe^{III}}$ -modified IRMOF-3 cube. The resultant catalyst displays an excellent electrocatalytic activity with high onset potential (0.93 V) and half-wave potential (0.78 V).[21a] $\mathtt{w u}$ and co-workers reported a highly active and stable Fe–N–C catalyst featured with well-dispersed atomic Fe in porous carbon matrix that underwent one single thermal conversion from Fe-doped ZIF-8.[41d] Compared to extensively studied ORR catalysts in alkaline media, the resulting catalyst also shows highly active and stable ORR performance in acidic electrolytes. Fe doping at an atomic level apparently increases the density of the ORR active $\mathrm{FeN}_{x}$ sites in a robust 3D architecture so that it shows high efficient ORR performance. Recently, Lai et al. constructed the FemIm nanocluster $@$ ZIF-8 precursors and further transformed it into the Fe–N/C electrocatalysts by the pyrolysis. The ZIF-8 host network exhibits a significant confinement effect for the formation of $\mathrm{FeN}_{x}$ active sites during the pyrolysis. Experimental results and density functional theory (DFT) calculations demonstrate that the determined $\mathrm{Fe-N}_{x}$ sites with five-coordinated configuration can significantly promote the ORR activity in acidic media.[21c] In addition, Strickland et al. used ZIF-8 as an encapsulator for Fe chelated with a nitrogen complex to synthesize an Fe-based catalyst, which shows high activity for ORR in acidic media.[41e] The above examples demonstrated that the introduction of Fe or Co into $Z\mathrm{n}$ -containing MOF is a promising strategy to improve ORR activity, especially under acidic conditions. In fact, except that the Zn-containing MOF can be considered as hosts, other metal-based MOFs can be also employed as hosts. For example, Bhattacharyya et al. used the $\mathrm{Mg^{II}}.$ -based anionic MOFs incorporated by trace amounts of metallic cobalt as a precursor to prepare a Co, N-doped porous carbon. The carbon catalyst exhibits excellent electrocatalytic ORR activity and stability, which is mainly attributed to the improved electronic interaction between metallic Co and the N-doped graphitic carbon matrix.[71] In addition, Tang et al. also reported a class of Co–MOF-derived dual metal (Co, Ni, Fe, Zn, and Cu) and nitrogen-codoped carbon catalysts. The CoNi–MOF-derived catalyst shows the best ORR performance with an onset potential of 0.347 V (vs SCE) in phosphate-buffered solution $(\mathrm{pH}=7)$ solution and excellent stability.[72] Zhu et al. adopted a double-phase encapsulation approach, in which the thiourea and $\mathrm{CoCl}_{2}$ were introduced into the MOF (MIL$101\mathrm{-NH}_{2}\mathrm{)}$ pores as precursors, to synthesize a honeycomb-like porous carbon nanostructures immobilized with $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}$ particles by pyrolysis. The obtained catalyst displays a comparable ORR catalytic activity, superior long-term stability, compared to commercial $20\ \mathrm{wt\%\Pt/C}$ under alkaline conditions.[73]
As mentioned above, Zn-, Fe-, and Co-containing MOFs have been extensively used as precursors to prepare high efficient ORR catalysts because it is commonly believed that Fe and Co provide the best activity sites. In fact, other metals (except Zn, Fe, and Co)—containing MOFs—can also be used as precursors to obtain the ORR catalysts. Most recently, our group used Cu-containing ZIF-8 as a precursor to prepare a $\mathrm{Cu-N-C}$ catalyst and further explore its ORR catalytic performance.[74] As shown in Figure 12a, Cu–ZIF-8 precursors were prepared by adding the synthesized copper nanowires to the ZIF-8 synthesis feedstock at room temperature. Then, the Cu–N–C catalyst was prepared by carbonization of Cu–ZIF-8 at $950~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{8\mathrm{h}}$ . The results indicate that the $\mathrm{Cu-N-C}$ catalyst nearly follows the four-electron route (the value of electron transfer of 3.92). Moreover, both the onset potential and limited current density of $\mathrm{Cu-N-C}$ are almost the same as the commercial $20\%$ $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ catalyst (see Figure 12b). Importantly, it exhibits a slightly better half-wave potential $(16~\mathrm{mV})$ than the $20\%\ \mathrm{Pt/C}$ catalyst. The excellent performance of ORR activity of Cu–N–C is mainly ascribed to following three reasons. First, $\mathrm{Cu-N-C}$ possessed sufficient and evenly distributed active sites. Second, $\mathrm{Cu-N-C}$ displayed an appropriate hierarchical porous structure. Third, with metal Cu inlaid, the enhanced electrical conductivity of $\mathrm{Cu-N-C}$ could facilitate the ORR activity. Similarly, Lai et al. fabricated the Cu, N-codoped ORR electrocatalyst by directly pyrolyzing Cu-doped ZIF-8 polyhedrons, and the obtained catalyst with a refined hierarchical porous structure, high surface N content, and $\mathrm{N-Co}^{2+}\mathrm{-Cu}^{0}$ active sites exhibits a high ORR activity and stability in $0.1~\mathrm{~M~}$ KOH solution and also shows excellent performance in a $Z\mathrm{n}$ –air battery.[8d]
## 4.3. Carbonization of MOF-Based Composite
## 4.3.1. MOF and 1D Substrate Composites
1D nanomaterials include nanowires, nanorods, or nanotubes. Li et al. used CNT-based MOF as precursor to prepare $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanocrystal embedded in N-doped mesoporous graphitic carbon catalyst.[75] The resulting catalyst exhibits similar catalytic activity but superior stability to the commercial $20\mathrm{wt\%}$ $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ catalyst for ORR, and a $1.50{\mathrm{~V}}$ onset potential for OER, making it a high-performance cheap bifunctional catalyst. In addition, Ahn et al. reported an Fe, N-embedded hierarchical carbon framework wired on 1D porous carbon nanotube, which was derived from a rationally designed composite structure. The composite structure was obtained by nucleation and growth of the mixture of ZIF-8, polydopamine, and Fe on the surface of porous tellurium nanotubes.[19a] The synergistic effects between the hierarchically porous 1D carbon structure and the embedded $\mathrm{FeN}_{x}\mathrm{C}$ active sites in the carbon framework lead to the superior ORR catalytic activity in both alkaline and acidic media. A rechargeable zinc–air battery also exhibits excellent charge–discharge overpotentials similar to the counterparts of $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ ORR electrode and $\mathrm{IrO}_{2}$ OER electrode. Recently, Ma et al. used the pine needle-like ZIF-67/cobalt carbonate hydroxide nanowire composites as a precursor to synthesize a Co-encapsulated N-doped porous carbon ORR electrocatalyst by pyrolysis. Due to high dispersibility of fine N-doped carbon-wrapped Co nanoparticles and the linear MOF-derived assemblies and high porosity, the catalyst exhibits a superior ORR activity with onset potential of $1.033\mathrm{~V~}$ and long-term stability in alkaline solution.[43b] Similarly, Manthiram and Ahn applied cobalt oxalate microtubes as 1D tubular structure for in situ synthesis of ZIF-67, which serves as a precursor to synthesize Co, N-doped carbon microtubular structure ORR catalyst composed of nanoscale hollow spheres and nanotubes. Because of the highly graphitized unique hierarchical tubular structure and stable ${\mathrm{Co-N}}_{x}$ moieties embedded into the carbon matrix, the carbon catalyst displays outstanding ORR activity even superior to $20\%$ Pt/C.[43c]
## 4.3.2. MOF and 2D Substrate Composites
Advantage of using Co-containing MOF composites as a precursor is that the resultant catalysts possess inherent features of both MOF and the substrate, and thereby increase the ORR performance of the catalysts. Wei et al. deposited the small size $(<20\ \mathrm{nm})$ ) ZIF-8/Co-ZIF-8 on the surface of graphene to synthesize N/(Co, N)-doped porous carbon/graphene catalysts via pyrolysis.[19b] The uniform coating of ZIF nanocrystals on the GO layer can effectively inhibit the aggregation of GO during high-temperature carbonization, and ZIF-8 or ZIF-67 can provide in situ ORR active sites after carbonization. The onset potential of the Co–ZIF-8–PC/rGO catalyst is $0.96\mathrm{~V~}$ under alkaline condition, slightly better than $20\%$ $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ . Furthermore, the Co–ZIF-8–PC/rGO catalyst also exhibits good ORR performance under acidic condition, and the onset potential of Co–ZIF-8–PC/rGO and $20\%$ Pt/C is 0.85 and $0.93\mathrm{~V},$ respectively. Similarly, Wei et al. adopted a seed-mediated deposition route to prepare MOF/GO/MOF sandwich-like nanostructure in which the core–shell MOF (i.e., ZIF-8 $\{\textcircled{a}$ ZIF-67) crystals were uniformly distributed on GO. Compared to the method of direct deposition of ZIF-67 crystals on GO, the seed-mediated synthesis can effectively prevent the inhomogeneous distribution of ZIF-67 crystals. Moreover, the obtained catalyst shows a high onset potential (0.93 V) and large kinetic current density $(101\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{mg^{-1}})$ at $0.80\mathrm{V}.^{[76]}$ Hou et al. fabricated the novel nitrogen-doped core–shell-structured porous $\mathrm{Fe}/\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{C}@\mathrm{C}$ nanoboxes supported on rGO sheets (N-doped $\mathrm{Fe/Fe_{3}C@C/r G O})$ by carbonizing graphene oxide and PB nanocube composite.[77] The N-doped $\mathrm{Fe}/\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{C}@\mathrm{C}$ and $\mathrm{Fe/Fe_{3}C@C/r G O}$ show high ORR activity with an onset potential of 0.91, $1.0\mathrm{~V~}$ and a halfwave potential of 0.83 and $0.93\mathrm{~V},$ respectively, indicating that a better ORR performance can be obtained by in situ introduction of NGO sheets.
Recently, Li et al. used CoAl–LDH nanoplatelets as a support to grow ZIF-67, and the obtained ZIF-67 $@$ LDHs were served as a precursor to synthesize a highly active catalyst by a pyrolysis. The catalyst exhibits the better electrocatalytic ORR performance compared to the counterpart obtained by pyrolysis of ZIF-67.[78] Moreover, Li et al. also used ZIF-67/ZIF-8 bimetallic MOF as precursors and LDHs as a supporter to prepare Codoped porous carbon.[25b] By controlling the composition of Co/ $Z\mathrm{n}$ to change the ratio of ZIF-67/ZIF-8, the obtained samples $(\mathrm{Co}/\mathrm{Zn}=2)$ ) give excellent ORR activity due to the most active sites and large porous structures (Figure 13). The onset potential of the catalyst reaches 0.967 V and the limiting current is $5.8\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ under alkaline conditions, which are slightly superior to commercial $20\%\ \mathrm{Pt/C}$ . A series of comparative experiments indicate that the increase of Co content stimulates the formation of graphitic carbon, implying the increase of graphitization degree, while the increase of $Z\mathrm{n}$ improves the content of nitrogen (Figure 13d).[25b] In addition, the structured 2D LDHs can be well maintained in the catalyst (see Figure 13b,c), which is helpful for the improvement of the ORR performance. Obviously, the obtained 2D carbon-based network would inspire us for rational design of catalysts.
## 4.3.3. MOF and Other Substrate Composites
Besides the MOF composite precursors discussed above, the mixtures of MOF with 0D, 3D, and other substrates have also been reported. Recently, Lou and co-workers used poly­ styrene $@Z\mathrm{IF}$ composite as a precursor to prepare unique Co/N-doped carbon nanocomposite hollow particles with a single hole in the shell. (Figure 14) During the thermal treatment, the 0D polystyrene core in these yolk–shell particles was thermally decomposed to generate gas outflux for the effective formation of large through-holes on the shells (Figure 14b,c). The ZIF-67 shells were easily converted simultaneously into cobalt/N-doped carbon microshells, composed of separated Co nanocrystallites inside porous carbon matrix. Remarkably, the $\mathsf{C o}/\mathsf{N C}$ sample shows higher ORR activity than the commercial $20\%$ Pt/C electrocatalyst in terms of half-wave potential (0.87 V vs $0.83\mathrm{V},$ Figure 14d).[79]
In fact, tailoring specific core–shell structures of the catalysts would greatly enhance ORR electrocatalytic activity. Xia et al. used ZIF-9 (Co) and multiwalled ordered porous carbon as precursors to fabricate the core–shell structure of $\mathrm{Co}@\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{C}$ at $700^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .[80] A notable feature of $\mathrm{Co}@\mathbb{C}\circ_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{C}$ is that the highly 3D ordered porous carbon can provide much better transport pathways than the individual MOF-derived disordered nanostructure, which would facilitate the mass transport of $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and an electrolyte. As a result, the well-designed $\mathrm{Co}@\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{C}-$ CM carbon catalysts show almost identical activity and superior stability as well as methanol tolerance for the ORR against the commercial $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ in alkaline medium, although the onset and half-wave potentials of the resultant catalyst are slightly negative than that of the $20\%\:\mathrm{Pt/C}$ .
In addition to the hard templates such as carbon materials, using organic compounds containing nitrogen as a soft template to synthesize electrocatalyst also is a hot topic. Su et al. synthesized the nitrogen-doped carbon nanotubes (NCNTs) via the direct solid pyrolysis,[81] and further found that mixing Zn–Fe–ZIF with proper amounts of dicyandiamide (DCDA) can finely tune $\mathrm{~N~}$ content, diameter, and formation temperature of the NCNTs. The results indicate that the addition of DCDA serves as the inducer of the graphitic structure and the extra nitrogen supplier since polycondensation of DCDA can act as a sacrificial template for the formation of graphite materials. The half-wave potential of NCNTs reaches $-0.183\mathrm{~V~}$ (vs SCE), which is even superior to commercial $20\%\:\mathrm{Pt/C}$ of $-0.190\mathrm{V},$ indicating that external DCDA source contributes significantly to the high electrocatalytic activity of NCNTs.
Wang et al. synthesized the M (Fe, Co)–N-containing ORR catalysts by pyrolysis of the composite of polyaniline (PANI) and prussian blue analogous (PBA) $(\mathrm{Co}_{3}[\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{CN})_{6}]_{2})$ .[82] They found that the addition of PANI nitrogen source greatly improves the ORR activity of the resulting samples. Compared to the PBA-derived catalyst, the introduced PANI effectively enhances the BET surface area and the micropores volume, and the metal-containing nanoparticles can be more homogeneously distributed in the PANI-rich samples. As a result, the resultant catalyst exhibits superior ORR performance in both alkaline and acidic electrolytes.
In this section, we have systematically reviewed the applications of MOF-derived heteroatom-doped porous carbon catalysts for ORR under alkaline and acidic conditions. For convenience, we also list the ORR electrocatalytic properties of MOF-derived heteroatom-doped porous carbons under alkaline conditions $(0.1\mathrm{~M~KOH})$ in Table 1 and under acidic conditions $(0.1\mathrm{~M~HClO_{4}}$ $0.5\mathrm{~M~H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4})$ in Table 2. Actually, the investigations on the ORR performance of the MOF-derived porous carbons under acidic conditions are relatively few. There are still great efforts needed to develop high efficient metal-free or nonprecious metal catalysts for ORR applications.
## 5. Conclusions and Perspectives
This review has systematically summarized the design methodology of MOF precursor, synthesis strategies of MOFderived heteroatom-doped porous carbon catalysts, and their applications in ORR. The importance of designing MOF precursors has been emphasized, because MOF precursors often determine the microstructures of the derived porous carbon catalysts and further affect their ORR performances. Generally, to synthesize high-performance metal-free carbon catalysts,
MOF-heteroatom carbonization and MOF-based composite carbonization methods are rather efficient, because the two methods can introduce more elements into the resulting samples and achieve the doping of nonmetal atoms in carbon matrix. However, to obtain high efficient metal (Co/Fe-based)- doped porous carbon catalysts, the design of the MOF templates becomes very important because the catalysts can be synthesized by the direct carbonization method.
Although MOF-derived heteroatom-doped porous carbon electrocatalysts have achieved great progresses, the following challenges still exist.
(1)	The synthesis of the MOF-derived porous carbon catalysts often involves high-temperature carbonization, which is very difficult to control or tailor the microstructure of the porous carbon samples. In addition, how to maintain the morphology and porosity of MOF precursor at high temperature is another challenge for the synthesis of a high-performance catalyst.
(2)	Currently, although the alkaline fuel cells have gained a great progress due to the low costs, their power density is lower by several times than those of the PEMFC in acidic media. Therefore, the development of high efficient nonprecious metal catalysts to replace precious metal Pt-based ones under acidic conditions is still a great challenge. It needs great efforts to design MOF precursors to synthesize high efficient ORR catalysts. In addition, the investigations on the ORR mechanisms and the active sites of the MOF-derived porous carbon catalysts under acidic conditions are also vital.
(3)	With respect to the MOF-derived metal-doped porous carbons, it is believed that metal doping would form the $\mathrm{M-N-C}$ bonds, which is favorable to ORR. However, metal doping also leads to the formation of other metal phase, such as metal/metal oxides, metal carbide encapsulated by graphitic carbons or carbon nanotubes. The active sites incurred by doping metal into porous carbons are not clearly understood yet. As a result, it is still very difficult to use MOF templates/precursors to tailor these active sites.
(4)	For the MOF-derived metal-free porous carbon catalysts, although researchers have extensively explored the effects of various types of nitrogen doping on the catalytic performance of the samples, the effects of the doping of the other nonmetal atoms, e.g., phosphorus, sulfur, boron, and fluorine, on the ORR activity of the resulting samples are still lacking. Only a few investigations have explored whether the other nonmetal atom doping would form different types of bonds with the surrounding carbons such as nitrogen, let alone the effects of different kinds of bonds on the ORR activity.
